<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.16. Putting it all together - Laplace's equation on a cylinder</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Last time we looked at a homogeneous system on a cylinder. 
      Today we're going to look at a final example just to see how
everything fits together. In particular, we'll consider a nonhomogeneous boundary conditions.  
</p>
<p>
  For the rest of this worksheet we will be focusing on the following problem:
  </p>
  <p>
  Consider Laplace's equation
<md>\nabla^2 u = 0</md> in a cylinder of radius <m>R</m> and height <m>H</m> where we
require <m>{u=0}</m> on the top and bottoms ``lids'' and allow <m>u</m> to be
possibly non-zero on the curvy side. Since this is Laplace's equation on a cylinder 
which represent the system at a steady-state, 
        time is not one of our variables and we want to use cylindrical coordinates, 
        so <m>{u = u(r, \theta, z)}</m>.
</p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>State the boundary conditions carefully.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Now we're going to separate variables so let's try
  <md>
  u(r,\theta,z) = S(r,\theta) Z(z).
  </md></p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>What differential equations do we find with separation of variables?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>How do the boundary conditions translate into conditions on either
  <m>S</m> or <m>Z</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Where is the Sturm-Liouville problem?  Is this a familiar
  Sturm-Liouville problem?  Find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let's move on the other equation. It ALMOST looks like something we've done before...</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Begin solving it by applying the
  separation of variables, letting <m>S(r, \theta) = R(r)\Theta(\theta)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Where is the Sturm-Liouville problem?  Is this a familiar
  Sturm-Liouville problem?  Find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>There is one final differential equation for us to solve.  What is it?  Is this a
  familiar equation?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>So close to familiar. Find the solutions to this equation that are bounded in the
  cylinder.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Put everything together to write the general solution of the
  equation.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose we know that <m>u(R,\theta,z) = f(\theta, z)</m>.  How would
  we find the solution?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
